# Project: GameTracker
A video game library and tracking app, conceptually similar to Letterboxd but for games.

## Stack
- React + Vite (web app, runs at localhost:5173 in dev)
- Capacitor wrapped for iOS (capacitor.config.json + ios/ folder exist) — this is also a native iOS app, not just web
- Game data sourced from IGDB API via a backend proxy in src/services/igdb.js (IGDB does not allow direct browser calls due to CORS; all IGDB requests go through our proxy with cached Twitch OAuth tokens)

## File structure conventions
- Pages live in src/pages/
- Reusable components in src/components/
- Custom hooks in src/hooks/
- API services and data layer in src/services/
- Shared styles in src/styles/, plus App.css and index.css at src/ root
- Types (if used) in src/types/
- App entry: src/main.jsx → src/App.jsx

## Design system
Tokens live in src/styles/tokens.css (spacing) and src/styles/theme.css
(everything else). DESIGN_SYSTEM.md documents them and is kept in sync with
the CSS — if the two ever disagree, the CSS is right and the doc is a bug.

- Dark theme: deep midnight navy (#0a0f1f) background, COBALT BLUE accent
  (#3b82f6 / --accent). Five bottom tabs: Home, Explore, Search, Library, Profile.
- Typography: DM Sans ONLY, at weights 400/500/600/700
  (@fontsource/dm-sans, imported in src/main.jsx). There is NO serif in this
  app. Display headers are DM Sans 600 with -0.02em tracking. --font-serif
  exists as a legacy alias but resolves to DM Sans — do not read it as a
  promise of a serif, and do not add "Georgia, serif" fallbacks.

### Spacing — value-named 4px grid
--space-4, -8, -12, -16, -20, -24, -32, -40, -48, -64. The token name IS the
pixel value. Anything off this grid is off-scale.
- --space-2 (2px) exists but is RESTRICTED: inline icon-to-text gaps only
  (glyph next to the word it belongs to). Never for padding, card gaps, or
  block-level spacing — round up to --space-4.
- The old t-shirt names (--spacing-xs/sm/md/lg/xl/2xl/3xl) are back-compat
  aliases onto the grid and are still correct in the ~2,000 places that use
  them. New CSS should prefer --space-*.
- Do not hand-roll raw px for margin/padding/gap/inset. ~855 existing
  declarations still do; that is a known backlog, not a pattern to copy.

### Type — seven roles, all DM Sans
Display XL 36 / Display L 28 / Heading 22 / Title 18 / Body 15 / Caption 13 /
Label 11. Use the .text-* utility classes (.text-display-xl, .text-display-l,
.text-heading, .text-title, .text-body, .text-caption, .text-label) rather than
hand-rolling font-size. Heading (22px) exists specifically to fill the old
18→28 gap — use it instead of inventing a 20/21/22px size.
Share cards and celebration screens are a deliberate carve-out: they render to
a fixed offscreen canvas (1080x1350 / 1080x1920) for image export and use the
separate --share-* tokens. Never mix the two scales in either direction.

### Retired palette — orange / amber / copper
The warm copper/amber palette is RETIRED and must not come back. It has drifted
back repeatedly, so it is now linted, not just a convention. Never hardcode a
hex in component CSS, and never introduce a warm/orange/amber/copper color.
The only sanctioned warm values are tokens already in theme.css: the opt-in
Ambassador copper accent, --star (rating gold), --tier-bronze / --tier-gold,
and --status-warning. If you think you need another one, add a named token to
theme.css and say why — don't inline it.

### Linting
`npm run lint` runs Stylelint over src/**/*.css in WARNING MODE (config:
.stylelintrc.cjs). It flags off-grid spacing values and hardcoded hex, with a
custom rule (scripts/stylelint/no-retired-palette.cjs) that catches the retired
palette both by known hex and by hue analysis. It does not fail builds yet, but
do not add new warnings; run it after touching CSS.

## Domain rules
- Game tracker statuses: Want to Play / Playing / Played / Dropped
- A game has at most one status at a time (mutually exclusive); do not implement as separate booleans
- All status changes must be optimistic — update UI immediately, then sync to store

## Conventions
- Match existing file structure; do not create new top-level folders without reason
- Use existing utility functions and components before creating new ones
- Plain JavaScript (.js / .jsx), not TypeScript — match existing code
- Use existing color tokens / CSS variables; do not hardcode hex colors
- Use --space-* spacing tokens; do not hand-roll raw px for margin/padding/gap
- Capacitor-aware: when adding interactive features (haptics, share, native menus), check if a Capacitor plugin exists before falling back to web-only APIs
- All animations must respect `prefers-reduced-motion`

## Reference docs in this repo
The repo has many .md files documenting prior work (DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, FEATURES_OVERVIEW.md, NAVIGATION_UPDATE.md, MOBILE_GRID_FIX.md, etc.). When making changes that overlap a documented area, read the relevant .md first to avoid contradicting prior decisions.

## Sprint 5 design language (Musicboard-inspired)
Pattern source: a music-tracking app called Musicboard. We are stealing
PATTERNS, not COLORS. GameTracker keeps deep midnight navy + cobalt accent,
DM Sans throughout (see the Design system section above — this section
originally said "copper/amber" and "serif display headers"; both are wrong).
Core patterns this sprint introduces:
- ReviewCard with a "cover header" -- game cover left, title +
developer right, tinted with the dominant color extracted from
the cover. Horizontal gradient: dominant color (40% opacity) left
fading to card surface right.
- FAB (Floating Action Button): bottom-right on Home only, cobalt
accent (--grad-brand), opens a bottom sheet for quick actions.
- Profile hero pattern: avatar + 3 stat numerals + name + bio +
social links + Follow / Send Message + inline tabs (Home /
Reviews / Lists).
- Search has 4 tabs: Games (default), Reviews, Users, Lists.
Per-tab Recent Searches with horizontal game covers on Games tab.
- Like / Comment / Share row on every review card. Like persists
locally via localStorage (Sprint 6 wires real backend).
- Profile sort sheet: 5 pill options -- Last Updated (default) /
Latest First / Oldest First / Most Liked / Alphabetically.
Backend status (CRITICAL):
- Sprint 5 is UI-only. Do NOT create new Supabase tables for
likes, comments, or follows -- Sprint 6 owns that.
- Likes persist via localStorage keyed 'gt:likes:v1'.
- Comments tap-through navigates to /reviews/:id/comments stub
page that says "Comments coming soon."
- Share uses Capacitor Share plugin if available, else Web Share API, else clipboard with toast.
- Follow button is visually present but no-op for now.
- Send Message routes to /messages/coming-soon stub.
Component reuse:
- Reuse the existing color extraction utility from Sprint 4 P8
(search the repo for useDominantColor or extractColorFromImage
before writing new code).
- Reuse the existing bottom-sheet component for FAB sheet, sort
filter, and any other modals (search src/components/ for
BottomSheet, Sheet, or Modal before writing new ones).

## Review card components — actual current state
This section used to say "ReviewCard.jsx is the canonical review card, do NOT
fork it." That was never enforced and is no longer true, so it was misleading
agents into assuming one component when there are several. Here is reality:

- src/components/reviews/ReviewCardShell.jsx is the shared layout primitive
  (bounded box, header, truncation behavior). It is what actually guarantees
  cards look consistent, and it IS the thing to reuse.
- src/components/ReviewCard.jsx — the full review card. Used by Profile,
  GameDetail, ReviewDetail, ReviewComments, Search, SearchOverlay,
  GameReviewsAll, DiscoverReviewsAll. Built on ReviewCardShell.
- src/components/home/HomeReviewCard.jsx — Home "pulse" feed and
  ReviewComments. A separate implementation (it renders unified activity
  items, not just reviews), also built on ReviewCardShell.
- src/components/explore/RecentActivityCard.jsx — Discover "Recently" shelf,
  via FollowingShelf. Also built on ReviewCardShell.

These three are NOT going to be merged into one component; they render
genuinely different content shapes. The rule that actually applies:

  Build on ReviewCardShell. Do not create a FOURTH review surface, and do not
  copy-paste one of the three above as a starting point — extend the one whose
  screen you're working on, with props.

Dead code, do not use as a reference or revive without a reason:
src/components/explore/ReviewFeedRow.jsx,
src/components/explore/ReviewOfWeekHero.jsx,
src/components/ProfileReviewsShelf.jsx — all three are unreferenced (nothing
imports them) and predate ReviewCardShell.